======================================================================
Part 1: K_B(h)[1](x) exact value
======================================================================
K_B(h)[1](x) = eps - eps*exp(-h/eps)
MATCHES eps*(1-exp(-h/eps)) -- residual 0. PASS
Positivity of the kernel e^{-v/eps}>=0 on [0,h] means this IS the
operator sup-norm of K_B(h) (attained exactly at f=+1), not merely
a bound on it -- standard fact for positive integral operators on
C_b: ||A|| = sup_x |A[1](x)| when A has a nonnegative kernel.

======================================================================
Part 2: T_w[1](x') exact value, from T_w's raw definition
======================================================================
T_w[1](x') = int_0^inf e^{-u^2/2-u*(x'+w)} du =: R(x'+w)   (by R's own definition)
i.e. T_w[1](x') = R( w + xprime )  -- definitional, not an assertion needing proof beyond R's def.

======================================================================
Part 3: the collapse identity x' + w = x + y = z
======================================================================
x' + w  with x'=x+v, w=y-v  ->   x + y
Equals x+y = z identically in v -- residual 0. PASS
(This is why T_{y-v} applied at the point x+v, for ANY v, always
 sees Gaussian-tilt parameter exactly z = x+y, independent of v.)

======================================================================
Part 4: K_A^raw(y,t)[1](x) exact value, from the raw definition
======================================================================
K_A^raw(y,t)[1](x) = Rz*eps - Rz*eps*exp(-h/eps)
MATCHES R(z)*eps*(1-exp(-h/eps)) -- residual 0. PASS
Since T_w has a nonnegative kernel (Gaussian-tilt e^{-u^2/2-u(.)}>=0)
and the v-integral weight e^{-v/eps}>=0 too, K_A^raw(y,t) is itself
a positive operator -- so this IS its exact operator norm, not a bound.

======================================================================
Part 5: ||M_y K_A^raw(y,t)|| exact value (M_y is a SCALAR)
======================================================================
M_y * K_A^raw(y,t)[1](x) = R_z*(1 - exp(h/eps))*(eps*z - 1)*exp(-h/eps)
||M_y K_A^raw(y,t)|| = |M_y| * ||K_A^raw(y,t)|| 
  = |(1-eps*z)/eps| * R(z) * eps * (1-exp(-h/eps))
  = |1-eps*z| * R(z) * (1-exp(-h/eps))        (eps cancels exactly)
Cancellation eps*|M_y| = |1-eps*z| confirmed symbolically -- residual 0. PASS

======================================================================
Part 6: K(y,t)[1](x) exact value (both pieces combined)
======================================================================
K(y,t)[1](x) = (1-exp(-h/eps)) * [ (1-eps*z)*R(z) + eps ]
Bracket (1-eps*z)*R(z) + eps  ==  R(z) + eps*sigma(z)   [sigma:=1-z*R(z)] -- residual 0. PASS

=> K(y,t)[1](x) = (1-exp(-h/eps)) * [ R(z) + eps*sigma(z) ]   EXACTLY

Cross-check against the ALREADY-CITED closed form (cu_direct_proof_
attempt Sec 3.1, wave 30 Sec 3.1): for f=1, f'=0 identically, so
rho(h',z)=0 and E_full(z)=0 in that decomposition, and the leading
term [f(x)-e^{-h/eps}f(x+h)]/z = (1-e^{-h/eps})/z exactly.
Since R(z)=(1-sigma(z))/z, R(z)+eps*sigma(z) = 1/z + sigma(z)*(eps-1/z),
Rewriting R(z)=(1-sigma)/z and simplifying:
R(z)+eps*sigma(z) == 1/z + sigma(z)*(eps-1/z)  -- residual 0. PASS
so K(y,t)[1](x) = (1-e^{-h/eps})/z + (1-e^{-h/eps})*sigma(z)*(eps-1/z),
matching leading term (1-e^{-h/eps})/z EXACTLY plus a remainder of
size O(sigma(z))=O(1/z^2) (already established via (G2) in the
